  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a workflow for studying communication between cells in the tumor stroma by combining human omics data with experimental model systems. On the left, single-cell RNA sequencing is used to profile cell populations, and these data are then used to predict receptor–ligand interactions, suggesting which cell types may signal to one another. Spatial transcriptomics adds a tissue-level layer, showing where gene-expression patterns are located within the sample.\n\nOn the right, the predicted interactions are tested in model systems using several standard assays. These include ELISA and western blotting to measure proteins or signaling changes, and migration assays to assess cell behavior. A second spatial component uses immunohistochemistry and fluorescence staining to visualize where relevant cells or markers are positioned in tissue.\n\nThe two streams converge at the bottom, where the results are integrated. The overall message is that computational inference from human single-cell and spatial datasets is paired with experimental validation and tissue imaging, allowing researchers to infer and verify crosstalk between different cell types in the tumor microenvironment. The emphasis is on linking molecular predictions with functional and spatial evidence.",
